<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Offenbach am Main</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Offenbach am Main is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, on the Main opposite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a>. It belonged to the counts, later princes, of Isenburg, who welcomed Huguenot refugees at the end of the 17th century, and it grew into an industrial town known above all for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leather">leather goods</a>. It is home to the German Leather Museum and the headquarters of the German Weather Service.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/offenbach-am-main.jpg" alt="Offenbach am Main" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Adrianflamind · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Offenbach_am_main_from_drone.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Offenbach am Main lies on the left bank of the river Main in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> and borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a>, of whose urban area it is part. Its inner city consists of the historic centre and the expansions of the 19th century. Three formerly independent suburbs were incorporated in the first half of the 20th century: Bürgel in 1908, then Bieber in 1938 and Rumpenheim in 1942. In 2019 the city council divided the whole city into 21 districts, where only nine had been officially districts before.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first documented mention of a suburb of Offenbach dates from 770, and a document of the Holy Roman Emperor Otto II of 977 gives the first mention of the place itself. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it passed through many hands. In 1486 Count Ludwig of Isenburg finally took control for his family, and in 1556 Count Reinhard of Isenburg moved his residence to Offenbach and built the Isenburg Palace, completed in 1559. It burned in 1564 and was rebuilt in 1578.</p><p>In 1635 Offenbach was given to the Landgraves of Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt">Darmstadt</a>, but it returned to the Isenburg-Birstein count, later prince, in 1642. It remained in that principality until 1815, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> gave the city to the Austrian Emperor Francis I. A year later it was passed to the Grand Duchy of Hesse-Darmstadt, and the monarchy there ended in 1918.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">French Protestants</a>, the Huguenots, came in the 17th century and helped to make the town prosperous. They brought knowledge of tobacco and turned Offenbach into a centre for rolling cigars. The town was more cosmopolitan than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a>, and Goethe and Mozart visited it several times. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> settled there from the late 16th century. Of 871 residents in 1829, the 40 Jewish families made up nearly a quarter of the population, and they had their own cemetery. The Jewish community was destroyed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a>.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> a third of the city was destroyed by Allied bombing, which claimed 467 lives. The city expanded to the south in the 1960s with the district of Lauterborn, and the office district of Kaiserlei was built on the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>In the 20th century the economy rested on machine-building, leather-making, typography and design, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/automobile">automobile</a> and pharmaceutical industries. Offenbach was a European centre of typography, with the firm Gebr. Klingspor, and the Linotype company, which produced the typefaces Optima and Palatino. Figures of traditional design included the typographer Rudolf Koch. The city has the German Leather Museum, the Klingspor Museum of typography and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calligraphy">calligraphy</a>, a design university and the German Meteorological Service. The Isenburg Palace is now used by the design university.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Offenbach am Main” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The pharmacy of Emanuel Merck in Darmstadt began industrial production of alkaloids in the 1820s and became a major chemical and pharmaceutical firm, Adam Opel started making sewing machines at Rüsselsheim in 1862 and motor cars in 1899, and <b>Offenbach</b> became known for leather goods.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-hesse">Grand Duchy of Hesse · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The territory was small and agrarian, with <b>Offenbach am Main</b> as its most important town, where Huguenot settlers had built crafts and trade.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-isenburg">Principality of Isenburg</a></li></ul></section>
